// Package migrations holds this application's schema migrations.
//
// Each migration is a file that registers itself from init, so importing this
// package is enough to make every migration known to the runner:
//
//	func init() {
//	    migrate.Register("20260101000000_create_users", up, down)
//	}
//
// Create one with:
//
//	ry make:migration create_users
//
// This package lives in the database overlay rather than the base skeleton
// because migrations are a SQL idea. The three that ship here create the tables
// the framework's own database-backed session, queue and cache drivers expect,
// so any SQL overlay has to carry an equivalent set; the MongoDB overlay
// carries none at all, and declares indexes from the models instead.
//
// They are written against GORM's migrator rather than raw SQL, which is what
// lets the same three files serve SQLite, Postgres and MySQL unchanged. Two
// MySQL-specific things are worth knowing before you add a fourth.
//
// # Index key widths
//
// InnoDB caps an index key at 3072 bytes and utf8mb4 spends four bytes per
// character, so 768 characters is the widest string column that can carry a
// plain index. Nothing shipped here had to be narrowed: the cache table's
// 255-character primary key and the users table's unique index on email come
// to 1020 bytes each, and the composite index on jobs (queue, available_at) to
// roughly 264. There is room, but not an unlimited amount of it — a
// uniqueIndex on anything wider than size:768 is where this bites, and it
// bites on MySQL alone.
//
// The older limit was 767 bytes, which 255 utf8mb4 characters already exceeds.
// It applies to MySQL 5.6 and to tables left on the COMPACT row format; 5.7
// and 8.0 default to DYNAMIC and to the larger limit.
//
// # Column types
//
// A []byte field with no size, as on the cache table's value column, becomes
// longblob here and bytea on Postgres. Both hold far more than a cache entry
// has any business holding, so the absence of a size tag is deliberate rather
// than an oversight.
package migrations
